<DocsPage
  title="Terminals, Processes & Scripts"
  description="Dev servers, watchers, named tasks, and interactive shells — long-lived work that outlives a single agent turn, managed in one place."
  currentPath="/docs/processes/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="processes">Processes</h2>
  <p>
    A process is a long-running service belonging to a project: a dev server, a
    database, a file watcher, a queue worker. Molt Code starts, stops, and
    monitors them, and keeps their logs where both you and your agents can read
    them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Manage them from the project toolbox, or from the CLI:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>moltcode processes list</code> — what exists and what is running.</li>
    <li><code>moltcode processes start|stop|restart &lt;id&gt;</code></li>
    <li><code>moltcode processes logs &lt;id&gt;</code> — recent output.</li>
    <li><code>moltcode processes grep &lt;id&gt; "pattern"</code> — search the logs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That last one is the one agents actually lean on. An agent debugging a
    failing request can grep the server log for the error rather than asking you
    to paste it — which is the difference between a useful investigation and a
    conversation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terminals">Terminals</h2>
  <p>
    Terminals are persistent interactive shells scoped to a project. Unlike a
    command run inside an agent turn, a terminal outlives the turn — so it suits
    REPLs, TUIs, watchers, and anything that should keep running while other work
    happens.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use a terminal when the thing you are starting should still be alive
    afterwards. Use a plain command inside an agent run when it should finish and
    report. Starting a dev server as a blocking command is the classic mistake:
    the turn never ends.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scripts">Scripts</h2>
  <p>
    Scripts are named project tasks — build, lint, test, codegen, seed, deploy.
    Configure them once and run them by name:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>moltcode scripts list</code></li>
    <li><code>moltcode scripts run &lt;id&gt;</code> / <code>stop &lt;id&gt;</code></li>
    <li><code>moltcode scripts status &lt;id&gt;</code></li>
    <li><code>moltcode scripts logs &lt;id&gt;</code> / <code>grep &lt;id&gt; "pattern"</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Defining your test and lint commands as scripts is worth the five minutes.
    It gives agents an unambiguous way to verify their own work, and "run the
    test script before claiming it works" becomes an instruction that means
    something concrete.
  </p>

  <h2 id="watching-output">Watching Output</h2>
  <p>
    Processes and scripts both stream output that persists, so you can start
    something, walk away, and read what happened. Agents use the same logs — an
    agent that just changed a component can watch the dev server's rebuild and
    catch a compile error without you noticing there was one.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Prefer grep to reading logs whole.</strong> A verbose dev server
      produces enormous output; searching for the error is faster for you and
      considerably cheaper for an agent than reading the entire tail.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsPage>
